Say where the toolbar landed against the content.

MoneyMoney draws its Preferences pane text straight through the toolbar item labels (#255), and
nothing said where either one was. The height itself looked correct on reading:
-[NSToolbarItem sizeForSizeMode:...] adds the label height for IconAndLabel and Default and the
min and max clamp happens before that addition, so the reported height should include the labels.
That left the placement, and placement needed numbers.

-[NSWindow _toolbarSizeDidChangeFromOldHeight:] now prints the old and new heights, the delta, the
toolbar origin it chose, the content view frame and the window size, gated on CIDER_TRACE_TOOLBAR.

It answered immediately. MoneyMoney's Preferences window, 406 by 300 content:

    CIDER_TOOLBAR old=0.0  new=24.0 delta=24.0 toolbarY=276.0 content={0,0 406x300} window=406x346
    CIDER_TOOLBAR old=24.0 new=56.0 delta=32.0 toolbarY=268.0 content={0,0 406x300} window=406x378

The content view never moves. It is pinned with NSViewNotSizable while the window frame grows, so
the space the window gains is entirely above it, and the toolbar is placed at
NSMaxY(content) - delta. With a content top of 300 and a toolbar 56 tall that puts the toolbar at
268 to 324, so it sits 32 pixels INSIDE the content rather than on top of it.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and I tried it: moving the toolbar to NSMaxY(content) does place it
correctly, the trace then reads toolbarY=300.0 for Preferences and 680.0 for the main window, and
THE VISIBLE OVERLAP IS UNCHANGED. So the placement is wrong, and it is not what the eye is seeing.
Something else decides where the labels and the pane are drawn. That change is therefore NOT in
this patch; only the instrument is, because a behaviour change on a path every window takes should
not land on the strength of reasoning when the measurement says it changes nothing.

Off by default: this runs on every toolbar size change.

Task #255.

--- a/AppKit/NSWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindow.m
@@ -1717,6 +1717,23 @@
     [self setFrame: frame display: NO animate: NO];
 
     [[self contentView] setAutoresizingMask: mask];
+
+    /* WHETHER THE CONTENT EVER MOVED. Only the toolbar origin is set here and the content view is
+     * pinned while the window frame grows, so a delta of zero on the call that matters leaves the
+     * toolbar sitting on top of the content. MoneyMoney draws its Preferences pane through the
+     * toolbar labels. Task #255. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_TOOLBAR") != NULL) {
+        NSRect content = [[self contentView] frame];
+
+        fprintf(stderr,
+                "CIDER_TOOLBAR old=%.1f new=%.1f delta=%.1f toolbarY=%.1f "
+                "content={%.1f,%.1f %.1fx%.1f} window=%.1fx%.1f\n",
+                (double) oldHeight, (double) newHeight, (double) contentHeightDelta,
+                (double) toolbarOrigin.y, (double) content.origin.x, (double) content.origin.y,
+                (double) content.size.width, (double) content.size.height,
+                (double) frame.size.width, (double) frame.size.height);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
 }
 
 - (void) setToolbar: (NSToolbar *) toolbar {
